# ============================================================================
# TAU-NOMIC TUTORIAL 03 - THE LIVING CONSTITUTION
# A constitution as a temporal machine that accumulates its own law
# ============================================================================
#
# Parts 1 and 2 were snapshots: single decidable questions. But a real
# constitution is not a snapshot - it LIVES. Rights get proposed, adopted,
# rejected; the legal situation at time t depends on everything that
# happened before. This part builds the minimal living constitution:
# a Tau `run` whose STATE IS THE LAW ITSELF.
#
# The idea in one line:
#
#   o0s[t] : tau     the constitution at step t - A SPEC AS A VALUE (part 1),
#                    carried through time and grown by decidable meets.
#
# THIS is what tau-nomic is at its core. Everything else (roles, votes,
# the ratchet) is built around this loop.
#
# Run me:  tau -q < nomic_03_living_constitution.tau
# Verified against: Tau 0.7.0-alpha, build 240788e (alpha syntax may drift).
# (The proposals at the end of this file ARE the session - the file
#  replays the whole constitutional history by itself. The run first
#  echoes one large normalized formula - scan past it for the
#  "o0res[t] := ..." and "o0s[t] := ..." lines; those are the story.)
# ============================================================================

# EXPECTED-CODES: 0,9,8,9,9

set charvar off

# ============================================================================
# 1. THE MACHINE, PIECE BY PIECE
# ============================================================================
#
# Input:   i1[t] : tau      the proposal at step t (a spec-value!)
# Outputs: o0s[t] : tau     the living constitution after step t
#          o0res[t] : bv[8] the verdict code for step t
#
# The whole legislative procedure is ONE formula:
#
#   o0s[0] = { o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 }          the ENTRENCHED CORE:
#                                            "destructive action o1 only
#                                             with authorization o4"
#
#   if (o0s[t-1] & i1[t]) != 0               is the proposal CONSISTENT
#                                            with everything in force?
#      then o0s[t] = o0s[t-1] & i1[t]        adopt: law grows by the meet
#           o0res[t] = 9                     verdict: adopted
#      else o0s[t] = o0s[t-1]                reject: law unchanged
#           o0res[t] = 8                     verdict: rejected
#      (a driving application may localize these - e.g. as the German
#       labels "aufgenommen" / "abgelehnt" - same codes, same machine)
#
# Three things to notice BEFORE it runs:
#
#   (a) The consistency check `(o0s[t-1] & i1[t]) != 0` is part 1's meet -
#       executed INSIDE the machine, at every step, on the machine's own
#       state. The constitution decidably reasons about itself while running.
#
#   (b) There is NO rule that can remove the core: every future state is
#       o0s[0] & ... & ... - the core is re-conjoined into everything,
#       forever. Rejection of core-breaking proposals is not a policy that
#       someone enforces; it is ALGEBRA (the meet would be 0).
#
#   (c) There is no judge process, no validator service, no admin key.
#       The `run` IS the institution.
#
# Why can no conventional system do this? Because (a) requires deciding
# satisfiability of its OWN rule-state as a value - for rule systems that
# can grow, that is Rice-undecidable in every Turing-complete setting.
# Tau can, because it traded Turing-completeness away (part 1).

# ============================================================================
# 2. WHAT YOU WILL SEE (the session below, annotated)
# ============================================================================
#
# The four proposals at the end of this file replay this history:
#
# step 0  (no input)   the core constitutes itself:
#         o0res[0] := 0
#         o0s[0]   := always o1[t]:tau' != 0 || o4[t]:tau' = 0
#         (that IS "o1 -> o4" - Tau displays the Boolean normal form:
#          "o1 is off, or o4 is on")
#
# step 1  proposal: o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1   ("every action o5 gets a log o6")
#         consistent with the core -> ADOPTED:
#         o0res[1] := 9
#         o0s[1]   := always (o1' != 0 || o4' = 0) && (o5' != 0 || o6' = 0)
#         The constitution GREW. Two rights now in force.
#
# step 2  proposal: o1[t]=1 && o4[t]=0   (THE ATTACK: "allow destruction
#         WITHOUT authorization" - the exact negation-in-spirit of the core)
#         meet with the core is 0 -> REJECTED:
#         o0res[2] := 8
#         o0s[2]   := unchanged (same as o0s[1])
#         No debate. No override. The attack is not forbidden by a guard
#         that could be bypassed - it is INCOMPATIBLE, as a matter of
#         arithmetic, with what is in force. Un-argue-away-able.
#
# step 3  proposal: o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1   (the SAME right again - a "Sybil
#         vote" from part 2)
#         consistent -> code 9, and the display shows the conjunct TWICE:
#         o0s[3]   := ... && (o5' != 0 || o6' = 0) && (o5' != 0 || o6' = 0)
#         HONEST NOTE: by idempotence (part 2) this VALUE equals o0s[2] -
#         but the printed form is the unnormalized accumulation. Tau does
#         not re-normalize the display; semantics and syntax differ here.
#         A production driver therefore short-circuits duplicates BEFORE
#         stepping the run: a decidable entailment check answers "already
#         in force" (verdict code 10) and the run is not stepped at all.
#         (Exactly part 2, query %3 - now you know why that code exists.)
#
# step 4  proposal: o7[t]=1 -> o8[t]=1   (a new, consistent right)
#         -> code 9, constitution grows to three (distinct) rights.
#
# After step 4 the file ends; the run stops waiting for input. In a full
# deployment this run never ends - it is the LIVING constitution, driven
# step by step by a host process. The verdict codes you saw ARE the
# machine's complete public interface; any frontend merely labels them.

# ============================================================================
# 3. THE MACHINE ITSELF - then the session
# ============================================================================

i1 : tau := in console

# NOTE ON FILE FORMAT: after `run` starts, every following line of this
# file is consumed as INPUT - even a blank line would eat a step and
# swallow the next proposal. No blank lines from `run` to end of file.
run ( (o0s[0]:tau = { o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 }) && ( ((o0s[t-1]:tau & i1[t]:tau) != 0) ? ((o0s[t]:tau = o0s[t-1]:tau & i1[t]:tau) && (o0res[t]:bv[8] = { #x09 }:bv[8])) : ((o0s[t]:tau = o0s[t-1]:tau) && (o0res[t]:bv[8] = { #x08 }:bv[8])) ) )
o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1
o1[t]=1 && o4[t]=0
o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1
o7[t]=1 -> o8[t]=1
